{
  "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
  "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0000-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 1:00 p.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0000-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The dramatic collapse of Enron had profound reverberations experienced throughout the Houston economy and citizenry."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: What do we take from trial counsel at the end of the voir dire process announcing that if he had had extra preemptory challenges, he would have used them only against 6 of the 12 people that were finally selected? If that's all he would have ejected, why couldn't a fair jury have been found?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "From the very outset, we complained about this process. We said at the outset before trial that no juror could be seated in this case because the process of voir dire couldn't adequately be relied upon in these conditions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our first order of submission is that the proceedings should have been transferred, not necessarily because there don't in fact exist or there didn't in fact exist 12 unbiased jurors in the city of Houston."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SRINIVASAN: To be clear, Justice Breyer, that ordinary practice would only be altered in the very rare category of cases that involve a wave of public passion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Can I look at --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DREEBEN: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: When Judge Lake approached this case with the question of how to select a jury, he had 15 years of experience in selecting juries, and he informed the parties that it was his experience that voir dire conducted by the trial judge is more effective at eliciting the potential biases of a juror than the oftentimes tendentious voir dire that\u2019s conducted by the parties."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Were -- were these colloquies that are reported, the -- the pages we have just been reviewing, heard by the entire jury pool?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DREEBEN: That's correct, Justice Kennedy. This was not a case like Mu'Min, where in your concurrence -- your dissent, you pointed out that the colloquy occurred in the full presence of the -- of every other juror, and there was no individualized voir dire. Here there was individualized voir dire. Judge Lake had that juror right in front of him, eyeball to eyeball, and was able to make the kind of credibility assessment, taking into account all of the context, that no other judge can do."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And it's not to say that there\u2019s no judicial review of that on appeal. In the Irvin case, Irvin v. Dowd, which is really the Court's first case in this line, the Court noticed that there -- 90 percent of the jurors had an opinion that the defendant was guilty. It involved a highly sensationalized murder in rural counties in southern Indiana. There was a barrage of pretrial publicity. Eight of the 12 jurors said they had an opinion that the defendant was guilty. The Court, after meticulously reviewing the voir dire, concluded that the judge had committed manifest error in accepting the representations of the jurors that they could be impartial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0002-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This very trial itself of Mr. Skilling resulted in nine acquittals on insider trading counts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now, in our view Petitioner has essentially conceded that the honest services statute is not vague as applied and, therefore, facially unconstitutional. He all but acknowledges that bribes and kickbacks, which constitute the bulk of pre-McNally honest services cases, can be defined with precision. There is not an unconstitutional vagueness in it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Well, suppose you have a statute that -- that makes it criminal to -- to do any bad thing, okay? Now it's clear that murder would be covered. All right? Nobody would say that murder is not covered by that. Does -- does that make the statute non-vague?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: What authority do I look to, to see that some employees are fiduciaries and others are not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Dreeben, would you clarify the issue that came up: Is the government's theory focused simply on the compensation or does it involve the sale of shares?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DREEBEN: It involves the sale of shares as well. That was part of the compensation, and it\u2019s linked to it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1394",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ee5947285efa1b9f1667007742209af3a9d04f1dd31f0c078fc5bc3234caf606",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ee5947285efa-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: So either this was too little or Martha Stewart was too much? (Laughter.)"
    },
    {
